      "A C string is a char array whose end is marked by a zero byte: 'abc' is really {'a','b','c','\\0'} — four bytes. Functions like strlen count until that terminator; a missing terminator means reading past the end. string.h offers strcpy/strcat (unbounded — dangerous, see the memory lesson) and their bounded cousins strncpy/snprintf. The safe professional default is snprintf into a sized buffer, checking its return value.",
      "Functions in C declare their contract up front: a prototype (int max_of(int a, int b);) tells the compiler the argument and return types before use. Arrays 'passed' to functions actually pass the address of their first element (they decay to pointers — the next lesson's core idea), which is why every array function also needs the length as a separate parameter: the function cannot know it.",
