    relevance: "The browser is the most-attacked program your users run, and JavaScript is its language. Reading a page's scripts — what data they touch, where they put it — is the entry skill for XSS analysis, web testing (Level 8), and understanding what a phishing page actually does.",
    concept: [
      "JavaScript runs in the page: let declares a changeable variable, const a fixed binding (prefer const; let when reassignment is real). Types are dynamic like Python's, with one infamous difference: == performs type coercion ('5' == 5 is true) while === compares strictly — professionals use === always. Functions are values: you can define function greet(n){...}, or const greet = (n) => ..., and pass them to other functions — which is how the browser calls YOUR code when things happen.",
      "The DOM (ব্রাউজারে পেজের কাঠামো) is the browser's live tree of the page: document.querySelector('#msg') finds an element, .textContent reads/writes its text safely (it can never become markup), and .value reads form inputs. Events connect user action to code: button.addEventListener('click', handler) runs handler on click. Everything dynamic on the web — menus, live search, chat — is this loop: event → handler → DOM change.",
      "Security framing from the first day: data has sources (URL parameters, form input, server responses — attacker-influenceable) and sinks (places data lands: innerHTML interprets it as code-bearing markup — dangerous; textContent treats it as inert text — safe). Most client-side bugs are a source reaching a dangerous sink without a thought in between. The existing browser-security lesson builds the full model; this lesson gives you the language to read it.",
    ],
    diagramTitle: "Source → handler → sink",
